# process.nextTick(() => {}); process.env.scan()
# The row of process.env says of its result that it is an object, and that it comes out of a cell of native code. The table describes the object in that cell: its prototype object, what lies under its names.
# So it is no object of a class that nobody knows. If the row counted as one that says nothing of what it hands out, objects of any class might exist: the call would find `scan` on the prototype object of the class of Bun.Glob.
# The control: result-out-of-a-native-cell-that-nothing-describes-lets-any-class-exist.ir.
# LIBRARY: own
# RUN: link functions mechanisms=all
# On Windows what lies in that cell is a proxy that a function of the library makes: it is not the object that the table describes.
# REQUIRES: not-windows
# CHECK: function f2 unreached

